  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Periods of rain and gusty winds through this evening.\n\nA sharp mid level trough will continue to advance east across the \nGreat Lakes through tonight. An associated surface low will steadily \ndeepen as it moves down the Saint Lawrence Valley tonight to the\nCanadian Maritimes Saturday. A strong cold front crossing the \nregion will exit by mid afternoon. The better moisture and \ndynamics will be out ahead of the front, but a strong right \nentrance region jet dynamics will lag behind the passage of the \ncold front, allowing for periods of light rain and showers to \ncontinue this afternoon and evening in wake of the cold front. \nThe strongest winds have already occurred across the area, but \nwesterly wind gusts of 20 to 30 mph will continue this afternoon\nbefore turning northwesterly and diminishing further this \nevening.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Much colder tonight through Saturday night with a \nfew wet snowflakes possible early Saturday morning.\n\nStrong cold advection will develop tonight in the wake of the \ndeparting low, with 850 mb temperatures dropping into the -5C to \n-8C range by Saturday morning. The cold air aloft will support \nincreasing lake induced instability overnight with a brief lake \nresponse, but short NNW fetch and a rapidly drying synoptic scale \nairmass will keep lake effect muted and brief. Lake effect and \nupslope rain showers will develop southeast of the lakes tonight, \nwith a few wet snowflakes possibly mixing in very late tonight \nthrough early Saturday morning before the lake effect showers \nquickly end by late morning. The best chance of a few snowflakes \nwill be across higher terrain, but no accumulation is expected.\n\nThe coldest airmass of the fall season so far will arrive for \nSaturday, with highs only in the 40s areawide, and even upper 30s \nacross the highest terrain despite some afternoon sunshine. \nTemperatures will drop back into the lower 30s on the lake plains \nand 20s well inland Saturday night, with frost/freeze conditions \nlikely. A freeze watch has been issued for counties where the \ngrowing season remains. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...An extended period of dry and warmer weather begins \nSunday and lasts most of next week.\n\nHigh pressure will drift from the Ohio Valley to southern New \nEngland Sunday, with southwesterly return flow quickly removing the \nbrief shot of colder air. Temperatures will rebound into the 55 to \n60 degree range by afternoon following the cold start.\n\nA strong ridge surface and aloft will then take up residence early \nnext week across much of the central and eastern CONUS, while a slow \nmoving cutoff low remains parked off the Carolina coast much of the \nweek. This pattern will support dry weather most, or even all of \nnext week with abundant sunshine and temperatures a little above \naverage.",
